\chapter{Clustering, PANs, and Distributed State}
\label{ch:clustering}

As computing environments shift from isolated monolithic hosts to hyper-connected Personal Area Networks (PANs), micro-edge clusters, and disaggregated NUMA topologies, operating systems must treat distribution as a first-class execution primitive.

While platforms like OpenHarmony \footcite{openharmony23} and Fuchsia \footcite{fuchsia_zircon} provide high-level distributed service buses over heavy user-space IPC, Telix unifies single-node multikernel partitions and multi-device PAN topologies under a single explicit capability-driven message-passing control plane.

\section{System Positioning: Heterogeneous Edge \& PAN Topologies}
\label{sec:clustering_positioning}

Telix scales seamlessly from sub-4~KiB memory-constrained sensor nodes running MIPS \texttt{PageGrain} hardware \footcite{mips_pra_6_02} up to high-core-count server partitions running RISC-V \texttt{Svnapot} superpages \footcite{riscv_priv_1_12}.

\begin{description}
    \item[\textbf{Personal Area Networks (PANs):}] Low-power wireless nodes (BLE, Ultra-Wideband, IEEE 802.15.4) act as remote peripherals within a unified distributed capability domain. Memory-constrained PAN nodes export localized peripheral endpoints while offloading heavy async continuation processing to primary nodes.

    \item[\textbf{Competitive Positioning vs. OpenHarmony / Fuchsia:}] OpenHarmony achieves multi-device integration by running full Android/Linux subsystems linked via RPC daemons. Fuchsia uses Zircon channels with explicit FIDL IPC interfaces. Telix eliminates the dual-runtime penalty by compiling uniform zero-copy Rust continuation channels locally, which dynamically wrap into lightweight networked packet frames when crossing physical transport boundaries.
\end{description}

\section{Distributed Filesystem Access State}
\label{sec:distributed_fs}

Telix eschews centralized VFS locks in favor of a capability-indexed, conflict-free replicated data type (CRDT) namespace, building on concepts from Plan 9 \footcite{pike90plan9} and modern distributed object stores.

\subsection{Capability-Bounded Distributed Caching}
Filesystem handles are represented as capability tokens containing object IDs, cryptographically signed access rights, and generational offset invariants.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Ephemeral Edge Caching:} Edge nodes cache read-only extent blocks in local sub-page pools.
    \item \textbf{Lease-Based Mutation:} Write operations require acquiring a transient, single-writer capability lease from the authority node. If a low-power PAN node loses connectivity, the lease automatically expires without stalling host execution.
\end{itemize}

\section{Distributed Network Access State}
\label{sec:distributed_net}

Networking in a Telix cluster is decoupled from physical interface ownership. Socket state machines operate as distributed continuations that can dynamically migrate between hardware network interface cards (NICs).

\subsection{Transparent Socket Migration}
Instead of pinning a socket to a specific node's TCP/IP stack:
1. Sockets are registered as virtual channels within the multikernel transport routing layer.
2. Packet processing queues are exposed as memory-mapped submission and completion rings (\texttt{io\_uring}-style).
3. When a device moves across physical PAN access points, the socket continuation state remains active while the underlying ring buffer transport re-binds to the new physical link layer without dropping protocol connection state.

\section{Process and Continuation State Migration}
\label{sec:task_migration}

Conventional operating systems struggle with process migration because raw virtual memory pages, register sets, and execution stacks must be frozen and transferred over the wire \footcite{clark05migration}.

